  "rule": "Generated, not curated: a glyph subscripts iff Unicode defines a modifier letter (Lm) or subscript digit (No) whose compatibility decomposition is <sub> of it — filtered, as the superscripts are, to bases this layout produces (or plain ASCII) and to BMP forms, so the astral Cyrillic subscripts of U+1E0xx (Unicode 16) are excluded as near-universal tofu. No exceptions: there is no ʔ/ʕ analogue and no subscript ɡ, so bare `g` does not subscript. Lives on the shifted twin of the superscript operator (⌥⇧z), so Shift flips a raised glyph to a lowered one."
 },
 "classes": {
  "about": "Definitions for the flags carried by entries in `marks`. Membership lives on each mark, never in this prose — a key can be reassigned without editing a word here.",
  "ipa": "Absent or true: the mark is on the official IPA chart, and appears on /chart and /chart.json. `false`: it is not, and `beyond` names the tradition it answers to. Non-IPA marks are fully typeable and are listed on /keys.",
  "beyond": {
   "tenant": "Not IPA and never was. Carried so the layout can also write real Latin orthographies and romanizations — Romanian, Vietnamese, Polish, German, Semitic transliteration. Tenants cohabit with IPA marks on the Option layer; they are not their neighbours' pairs.",
   "tradition": "Standard within a phonological tradition but absent from the IPA chart — e.g. the Korean fortis mark. Real notation, wrong chart.",
   "extIPA": "Extensions to the IPA, for disordered speech (the 2015 chart). Not IPA proper, but the same transcribers need it — cleft palate, lisps, denasality. Flagged ipa:false like any other tenant."
  },
  "arbitraryKey": "The key claims the mark by neither phonetics, shape, nor convention. Assigned to a mediocre reach and labelled arbitrary rather than given a fake mnemonic.",
  "shiftSense": {
   "about": "What ⌥⇧ means for a mark that has a `double`. The sense is a property of the pair, not a single rule: six exist. Orthogonal to `exclusive`, which says whether the two forms replace each other.",
   "greater": "⌥⇧ is the greater pole of one dimension: advanced, raised, more rounded, ATR, upstep.",
   "extreme": "⌥⇧ is the more extreme value on a scale: extra-high, extra-low.",
   "lesser": "⌥⇧ is the diminished value: secondary stress, half-long. The mirror of `greater`, and the reason ⌥⇧ cannot be read as 'more' alone.",
   "below": "⌥⇧ is a DIFFERENT diacritic that shares this glyph's shape, sitting below the base: tilde/creaky, diaeresis/breathy, caron/voiced, dot/dot, breve/non-syllabic. The shape is a memory hook, not a meaning — ⌥⇧n is not 'a lower tilde', it is creaky voice.",
   "twin": "The two forms are independent — a shape or kin relation, no polarity. They stack. (ʿayn/hamza, undertie/‖, and the two voicing brackets, which are one system split across the ( and ) keys.)",
   "arbitrary": "An unpolarized dual: the two forms are values of one dimension but neither is greater. Which one takes ⌥⇧ is a coin flip, and is labelled as such. (apical/laminal.)",
   "placement": "⌥⇧ is the SAME mark on the other side of the base. The meaning is identical; placement is typography and the transcriber owns it (laws.placement). The direction differs per mark because each follows its own frequency: the ring defaults ABOVE (ŋ̊ j̊ ɡ̊, and ABC Extended's ring key), the syllabic line BELOW (n̩ l̩ m̩ r̩)."
  },
  "exclusive": "The two forms are values of the SAME dimension, so ⌥⇧ replaces rather than stacks — nothing is both advanced and retracted. Marks on INDEPENDENT dimensions stack (nasalized + creaky). Tone level is a five-way scale, so an exclusive pair need not be binary or exhaustive."
 },
 "optShift": {
  "about": "Deliberate ⌥⇧<digit> spends. The raw-US digit escape is fully retired: no shifted digit is an IPA glyph any more (the number-row roots are unshifted-digit digraphs, and the tie bar lives on ⌥j), so every ⇧<digit> types its native symbol directly. An unlisted ⌥⇧<digit> passes to the host — ⌥⇧2 €, ⌥⇧8 °, ⌥⇧9 ·, ⌥⇧0 ‚ all survive. Only the slots listed here are spent.",
  "1": "¡",
  "3": "ʿ",
  "2": "ʾ"
 }
}
